{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778549861,"nonce":"8dASUAxuyXSPdpCf0wr5mmH2v/A=","message":"**Update from channel-event testing (2026-05-11)**\n\nNew findings relevant to this issue's scope:\n\n1. **`allowedChannelPlugins` in settings.json**: Claude Code has an `allowedChannelPlugins` setting. When a channel is listed there, `--channels server:\u003cname\u003e` works without the interactive warning prompt that `--dangerously-load-development-channels` shows. Mandible's auto-registration should add `mandible` to this list in addition to registering the MCP server in `~/.claude.json`.\n\n2. **`--channels` flag**: The MCP server registration (`claude mcp add`) is necessary but not sufficient for channel events. The `--channels server:mandible` flag must also be passed when launching Claude Code, otherwise channel events (`notifications/claude/channel`) are silently dropped. This means auto-registration needs to cover BOTH the MCP server AND the channel loading.\n\n3. **cx wrapper belongs in Mandible**: The `cx` script in dotfiles wraps `claude` with `--channels server:mandible` (among other flags). This coupling between dotfiles and Mandible is fragile. Consider:\n   - `mandible wrap --app claude --channels` could inject the `--channels server:mandible` flag automatically when Mandible's channel server is enabled\n   - Or `mandible claude` as a first-class subcommand that launches Claude Code with the correct channel configuration\n   - The settings UI toggle (already in this issue's scope) would control whether the channel flag is injected\n\n4. **Settings-driven channel control**: When the settings UI toggle (req 3 in the issue body) disables the channel, `mandible wrap` should stop injecting `--channels`. This makes the toggle fully effective without requiring the user to also edit their shell wrapper.\n\nCross-ref: dotfiles `9c8daf7` (the cx changes that surfaced these findings).","files":null}]}